>To input normal text in lojban of course. It's standard lojban. And space =
is a symbol.
>
>
>
>

If stress is always indicated, be it by accented characters or capital lett=
ers, then the space character is unnecessary, but aids in readability. The =
comma is the same: if one understands Lojban morphology, then the comma is =
unnecessary, as it does not alter the pronunciation of a word, and two word=
s differing only in a comma are the same word.

That being said, the absolute minimal set of characters needed to write Loj=
ban using the Latin alphabet are the lowercase letters a to z, minus h, q, =
and w. The apostrophe and the dot are also needed. In case of atypical stre=
ss patterns (which only occur in cmevla) five accented characters are also =
needed (perhaps six, as I can't recall if one is allowed to stress ybu in c=
mevla). However, I would not consider each individual accented character to=
 be a separate character, as the sound is the same modulo the stress differ=
ence.

Unless we always indicate stress, the space is needed.

That's 25 letters + one symbol if we choose to not always indicate the stre=
ss, not counting the accented vowels as characters in their own right (whic=
h, IMO, they are not.)

.i mi'e la tsani mu'o
